  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Friday...\n\nAn upper low moving onshore into the Pac NW is causing the ridge\naxis over the region to slowly shift eastward, bringing an\nincrease in moisture from the south (PWATs 150-200% of normal). A\nshortwave moving into the region from the southwest will bring \nscattered showers and thunderstorms to the area during the \nafternoon and evening, gradually decreasing into the late evening\nand overnight. Convection will linger into the early morning hours\nacross the far east, where a low level jet is expected to develop.  \nThere could be a couple strong storms, with strong winds and small\nhail. With the high PWATs, any thunderstorm will be capable of \nproducing heavy rainfall. \n\nThe upper low pushes east along the US/Canada border Sunday into \nMonday, breaking down the upper ridge. As the low slides east, an\nassociated cold front is expected to push through the area \nlate Sunday. Areas west of Billings could see a period of \nelevated fire weather concerns on Sunday as pre-frontal conditions\nbring westerly wind gusts of 15-30 mph, with humidity down near \n20 percent. Another round of scattered showers and thunderstorms\n(30-60% chance) look to spread across the area during the \nafternoon and evening as well. A few of the thunderstorms could be\nsevere with modest instability (500-1000 J/kg) and shear (deep\nlayer shear around 30 kts). The main threats with the\nthunderstorms are strong wind gusts of 60+ mph and quarter size\nhail. Heavy rainfall is possible with the thunderstorms as well.\nThe SPC currently has a marginal to slight risk of severe\nthunderstorms Sunday afternoon and evening for most of the area.\n\nBehind the cold front, Monday will be mostly dry and a few degrees\ncooler than Sunday. Upper level ridging then builds in by\nTuesday, but weak energy and slightly above normal moisture \n(PWATs around 110-140% of normal) will allow for periodic chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms to continue through the end of the \nwork week. While uncertainty exits, another break down of the\nupper ridge could occur by late next week.\n\nHighs are forecast to \nrange from the 90s most places on Sunday, cooling into the middle \nto upper 80s Monday and Tuesday. A gradual warming trend is then\nforecast into the second half of the week with temperatures in the\nupper 80s to lower 90s. STP",
